  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nToday-Wednesday: Showers and Storms:\n\nA line of showers and storms remains ongoing from eastern South\nDakota into central and northern Minnesota. This line and it's\nassociated cold frontal boundary will shift east through the \nearly morning hours whilst weakening evident in decreasing \ntheta-e advection and instability through the morning. Resultant\nlocal impacts will be limited to scattered showers and storms \nby the time this line reaches the local area (09Z - 11Z), \nlasting through late morning into the early afternoon. Through \nthe afternoon and evening the scattered shower and storm threat\nlingers locally along and south of I-90 due to residual \nmoisture advection. The further away the front moves, the \nmoisture advection will follow; by Wednesday afternoon situated \nacross southern Iowa. \n\nWhile earlier runs supported a heavy rain potential across northeast \nIowa and far southwest Wisconsin, a consistently southern \ndProg/dt trend continued in most recent model runs (04.00Z) with\nthe majority of heavy rain now south of the local forecast \narea. The 00Z HREF gives a 30 to 60% chance for greater than \n0.25\" falling for far western portions of our CWA, from Olmsted \ndown south through Chickasaw counties and then east into far \nsouthwest Wisconsin. The highest probabilities are our southern \nrow of counties from Floyd county over to Grant county. Our \nforecasted rain amounts through Wednesday are now 0.1\" or less \nfor most areas north of I-90, then 0.1 to 0.7\" for areas south \nof I-90 with the highest amounts in far southern Grant, Clayton,\nand Fayette counties. Due to some instability remaining in the \narea, 500 to 1500 J/kg of MUCAPE, these rain totals could easily\nchange if a heavier shower or storm moves over a given \nlocation. Additionally the PWATs will also remain in the 1.5 to \n2.0\" range so this will help in increasing precipitation amounts\nif it is fully realized, however the probabilities would say \nthis is unlikely.\n\nThursday-Friday: Seasonable and Mostly Dry\n\nBy Thursday, surface high pressure builds in, giving the area a \nbreak from any precipitation chances. On Friday another shortwave \ntrough comes down into the Upper Midwest and while most of the day \nappears to be dry, there is increased precipitation chances (10 to \n30%), however with increased southwest flow in the low levels, there \nis lower confidence on any surface forcing that would allow \nprecipitation to reach the ground. With the southwest flow, \ntemperatures will also increase a little, more towards the upper 70s \nto upper 80s. \n\nThis Weekend: Increasing Warmth with Additional Storm Chances \n\nBy the time the weekend rolls around, zonal to northwesterly flow \nestablishes itself into the region with continued southwesterly \nsurface flow. Consequently, temperatures are expected to steadily \nwarm through the weekend with the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) inter-quartile spread ranging from the middle 80s to near \n90F by Sunday for high temperatures across the region. With the \nzonal to northwesterly flow in place, the key question that will \nneed to be monitored for over the weekend is how far northeast does \nthe ridge situated west of the local area build. Currently, there \nare some respectable probabilities (20-40%) for 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE \nand 30 kts of shear, so there is certainly some potential for severe \nweather when also considering AI guidance (CSU/Pangu/FengWu) does \npaint some probabilities (5-15%) as well. That being said, it will \nbe highly contingent on how quickly the ridge builds in, should it \nprogress into the region faster, this would decrease any convective \npotential. Regardless, thinking the low to medium-end precipitation \nprobabilities (20-50%) in the NBM for this weekend seem appropriate \ngiven the uncertainty of how this weekend may evolve.",
